    "text": "# `merge_intervals` Implementation\n\n```python\ndef merge_intervals(intervals):\n    \"\"\"\n    Merge overlapping closed integer intervals.\n\n    Args:\n        intervals: iterable of (start, end) pairs, inclusive on both ends.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new sorted list of merged (start, end) tuples.\n        The input is not mutated.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not intervals:\n        return []\n\n    # Copy + sort so we never touch the caller's data.\n    ordered = sorted((tuple(iv) for iv in intervals), key=lambda iv: (iv[0], iv[1]))\n\n    merged = [ordered[0]]\n    for start, end in ordered[1:]:\n        last_start, last_end = merged[-1]\n        # Closed intervals overlap/touch when start <= last_end + 1\n        # (use start <= last_end if you only want strict overlap).\n        if start <= last_end + 1:\n            if end > last_end:\n                merged[-1] = (last_start, end)\n        else:\n            merged.append((start, end))\n\n    return merged\n```\n\n## Invariant\n\nAt the top of each loop iteration, `merged` is a list of **pairwise disjoint, strictly increasing, non-adjacent** intervals that exactly cover the union of all intervals processed so far. Concretely:\n\n1. **Sorted & disjoint:** for every `i`, `merged[i].end + 1 < merged[i+1].start`.\n2. **Coverage:** `\u22c3 merged == \u22c3 processed",
